{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1557945192,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWU0NDQ1ODQyODc=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3419","origin":"github"},"title":"Destiny of `publish --missing`","message":"I was trying to get myself to like what `publish --missing` aims to do, but I keep failing on that. All it can to is nothing (`skip`), or call `create_sibling` (`inherit`). It fails for any other kind of remote, and of course cannot do anything if a superdataset has nothing in common with a subdataset in terms of  remotes.\n\nThat all comes at the price of an API argument slot, and a TODO list that nobody has tackled: #1344, #1491, #1673 in two years.\n\nI am not convinced that mixing tasks (push content vs. create publication targets) is anyhow superior to a dedicated command whose purpose is to create publication targets for datasets that don't have one. It certainly runs counter to my desire to come up with a reasonably minimal core API.\n\nAnother aspect is the implied dependency of `publish` on `create_sibling`. Taking one step back, I'd  consider `create_sibling` as more of a leaf command, as there are plenty of publications targets that have nothing to do with this functionality (all kinds of web/storage services) and even within the Git world (GitHub, GitLab) `create_sibling` is pretty much irrelevant.\n\nTaken all together, I think we should not fold `create_sibling` functionality into `publish`.","files":null}]}